1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:03,603
Imagine describing an engineering problem
in plain

2
00:00:03,603 --> 00:00:06,045
language, and having an AI help you

3
00:00:06,045 --> 00:00:08,066
build a numerical simulation.

4
00:00:09,087 --> 00:00:11,892
We'll use DeepSeek to plan the work.

5
00:00:12,813 --> 00:00:15,097
OpenCode coordinates execution.

6
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:19,665
The Model Context Protocol connects the
tools.

7
00:00:20,807 --> 00:00:23,611
The key is to keep three responsibilities

8
00:00:23,671 --> 00:00:24,092
clear.

9
00:00:25,335 --> 00:00:26,157
AI plans.

10
00:00:26,921 --> 00:00:28,930
Numerical solvers compute.

11
00:00:29,794 --> 00:00:31,538
Researchers validate.

12
00:00:33,221 --> 00:00:36,166
AI can prepare files and coordinate a

13
00:00:36,166 --> 00:00:40,252
calculation. The solver solves the
mathematical model.

14
00:00:40,873 --> 00:00:42,997
That alone does not prove the model

15
00:00:42,997 --> 00:00:44,279
is physically correct.

16
00:00:45,261 --> 00:00:48,931
We still check assumptions, units,
boundary conditions,

17
00:00:49,373 --> 00:00:51,238
and whether the results make sense.

18
00:00:52,221 --> 00:00:54,247
The goal is a workflow that's easier

19
00:00:54,247 --> 00:00:57,135
to use, easier to reproduce, and easier

20
00:00:57,135 --> 00:00:57,917
to inspect.

21
00:00:59,141 --> 00:01:01,426
Let AI help with the work.

22
00:01:01,987 --> 00:01:05,575
Keep scientific judgment and evidence at
the

23
00:01:05,575 --> 00:01:06,016
center.

24
00:01:07,541 --> 00:01:10,446
An AI answer can sound convincing,

25
00:01:10,747 --> 00:01:12,489
and still be physically wrong.

26
00:01:12,890 --> 00:01:15,535
Before we use AI agents for science,

27
00:01:15,816 --> 00:01:17,819
we need to understand that distinction.

28
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:20,882
Probabilistic and generative tools,

29
00:01:21,282 --> 00:01:23,423
from ChatGPT and DeepSeek

30
00:01:23,423 --> 00:01:26,365
to neural networks and stochastic
optimizers,

31
00:01:26,486 --> 00:01:29,427
help us recognize patterns, generate code,

32
00:01:29,648 --> 00:01:31,509
and propose candidate solutions.

33
00:01:31,889 --> 00:01:34,291
Their strength is exploring possibilities.

34
00:01:34,671 --> 00:01:37,473
Physical correctness still has to be
established.

35
00:01:38,233 --> 00:01:40,436
This becomes especially important

36
00:01:40,797 --> 00:01:42,058
in complex systems,

37
00:01:42,499 --> 00:01:44,621
where many particles interact

38
00:01:44,902 --> 00:01:47,886
and an analytical solution may not exist.

39
00:01:48,687 --> 00:01:51,329
Numerical solvers have a different job.

40
00:01:51,489 --> 00:01:55,152
Methods such as FDTD and RCWA,

41
00:01:55,512 --> 00:01:58,114
and circuit simulators such as SPICE,

42
00:01:58,415 --> 00:02:00,116
use mathematical models

43
00:02:00,276 --> 00:02:01,877
and established algorithms

44
00:02:02,137 --> 00:02:04,259
to calculate reproducible results

45
00:02:04,259 --> 00:02:05,780
under controlled conditions.

46
00:02:06,581 --> 00:02:08,844
But reproducible does not automatically

47
00:02:08,844 --> 00:02:09,825
mean accurate.

48
00:02:10,326 --> 00:02:12,670
We still need numerical verification:

49
00:02:13,131 --> 00:02:14,933
are the equations solved correctly?

50
00:02:15,434 --> 00:02:16,756
And physical validation:

51
00:02:16,756 --> 00:02:18,779
does the model represent the real system?

52
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:20,842
So here is the key message.

53
00:02:21,262 --> 00:02:22,744
Let AI propose.

54
00:02:23,104 --> 00:02:24,425
Let solvers compute.

55
00:02:24,586 --> 00:02:26,047
And let scientists validate.

56
00:02:26,488 --> 00:02:27,969
By combining these strengths,

57
00:02:28,009 --> 00:02:30,231
we build a more reliable scientific
workflow,

58
00:02:30,392 --> 00:02:31,933
without relying on AI alone.

59
00:02:34,214 --> 00:02:36,876
Why use deterministic numerical solvers

60
00:02:36,896 --> 00:02:39,359
in an AI-assisted scientific workflow?

61
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:42,042
Because a useful answer must also be

62
00:02:42,102 --> 00:02:45,546
reproducible, inspectable, and physically
defensible.

63
00:02:46,346 --> 00:02:48,088
First, reproducibility.

64
00:02:48,468 --> 00:02:50,590
With the same inputs, solver version,

65
00:02:50,591 --> 00:02:51,772
and numerical settings,

66
00:02:52,072 --> 00:02:53,914
we should reproduce the calculation

67
00:02:53,914 --> 00:02:55,555
within an appropriate tolerance.

68
00:02:55,896 --> 00:02:56,997
Record those conditions

69
00:02:56,997 --> 00:02:58,939
so another researcher can repeat the work.

70
00:02:59,560 --> 00:03:00,881
Second, transparency.

71
00:03:01,221 --> 00:03:03,502
We can inspect the governing equations,

72
00:03:03,722 --> 00:03:05,443
boundary conditions, solver logs,

73
00:03:05,603 --> 00:03:07,004
and convergence behavior.

74
00:03:07,404 --> 00:03:09,165
That gives us an evidence trail,

75
00:03:09,185 --> 00:03:10,766
rather than asking a language model

76
00:03:10,766 --> 00:03:12,326
to invent a numerical result.

77
00:03:12,846 --> 00:03:14,167
Third, portability.

78
00:03:14,747 --> 00:03:17,949
Many open-source solvers can run on a
laptop,

79
00:03:18,289 --> 00:03:19,670
a laboratory server,

80
00:03:19,810 --> 00:03:22,031
or a high-performance computing cluster.

81
00:03:22,531 --> 00:03:25,052
The right platform depends on the
workload.

82
00:03:25,894 --> 00:03:27,195
Fourth, education.

83
00:03:27,436 --> 00:03:29,319
Students should see how the physics,

84
00:03:29,319 --> 00:03:31,562
assumptions, and numerical methods

85
00:03:31,863 --> 00:03:32,904
produce a result.

86
00:03:33,125 --> 00:03:35,769
An inspectable workflow makes those
choices

87
00:03:35,809 --> 00:03:37,632
easier to understand and question.

88
00:03:38,353 --> 00:03:40,954
But deterministic does not automatically

89
00:03:40,954 --> 00:03:41,895
mean accurate.

90
00:03:42,035 --> 00:03:43,696
Open-source tools still require

91
00:03:43,696 --> 00:03:46,277
version pinning, mesh and convergence
checks,

92
00:03:46,457 --> 00:03:47,858
and physical validation.

93
00:03:48,058 --> 00:03:50,239
Commercial software can also be the better
choice

94
00:03:50,239 --> 00:03:51,739
for certification workflows.

95
00:03:52,540 --> 00:03:54,681
The division of responsibility is simple.

96
00:03:55,102 --> 00:03:56,822
AI plans and coordinates.

97
00:03:57,083 --> 00:03:58,803
Numerical solvers calculate.

98
00:03:59,064 --> 00:04:00,844
Scientists verify and validate.

99
00:04:01,025 --> 00:04:03,066
Together, these roles make AI-assisted

100
00:04:03,066 --> 00:04:04,926
scientific computing more trustworthy.

101
00:04:07,366 --> 00:04:11,626
In the architecture shown here, DeepSeek helps with

102
00:04:11,626 --> 00:04:14,586
planning, and OpenCode organizes files

103
00:04:14,586 --> 00:04:15,906
and tool execution.

104
00:04:16,466 --> 00:04:20,406
The model context protocol, or MCP, connects the

105
00:04:20,406 --> 00:04:24,206
application to callable tools, those tools

106
00:04:24,206 --> 00:04:25,386
invoke the solver.

107
00:04:25,806 --> 00:04:27,706
I then inspect the return evidence.

108
00:04:28,206 --> 00:04:30,546
The protocol itself does not calculate the physics.

109
00:04:33,046 --> 00:04:35,786
The working loop is to plan, prepare, run,

110
00:04:36,046 --> 00:04:36,826
inspect and report.

111
00:04:37,006 --> 00:04:38,126
Before accepting a result,

112
00:04:38,306 --> 00:04:40,766
I asked whether units are consistent, whether a

113
00:04:40,766 --> 00:04:43,266
simple reference case agrees, and whether numerical

114
00:04:43,266 --> 00:04:44,866
refinement changes the conclusion.

115
00:04:45,226 --> 00:04:46,906
A failed check belongs in the report.

116
00:04:47,186 --> 00:04:48,226
It should never disappear

117
00:04:48,226 --> 00:04:49,746
behind a polished animation.

118
00:04:52,426 --> 00:04:55,846
Before trusting an impressive image, ask for its

119
00:04:55,846 --> 00:04:56,626
inputs and

120
00:04:56,626 --> 00:04:57,826
one meaningful check.

121
00:04:58,386 --> 00:05:01,346
Let the assistant coordinate the workflow, let numerical

122
00:05:01,346 --> 00:05:02,926
tools produce traceable

123
00:05:02,926 --> 00:05:05,706
evidence, and keep responsibility for interpretation.

124
00:05:06,346 --> 00:05:07,886
You can apply that habit immediately,

125
00:05:08,266 --> 00:05:10,606
choose one result, and see whether someone else

126
00:05:10,606 --> 00:05:12,806
could reconstruct the chain that produced it.
